{
  "_README": [
    "The authoritative expansion for every acronym the four courses use.",
    "",
    "`expand` is what gets written into a slide at the acronym's first use. Lower case unless the",
    "term is a proper noun, a standard's name, or a credential, because that is how the decks",
    "already write the ones they do expand (electronic codebook, not Electronic Codebook).",
    "",
    "`literal` lists tokens the scanner sees as acronyms that are not acronyms at all and must never",
    "be expanded: MITRE technique IDs, OWASP category IDs, CPU registers and flags, hex byte values,",
    "PE section and header signatures, file extensions, exam and course codes, learning objective",
    "labels, table row labels, and names that no longer stand for anything (YAML, QUIC, ISACA, GNU).",
    "Writing 'MZ (Mark Zbikowski)' onto a slide would be true and useless; writing an expansion for",
    "T1055 would be false.",
    "",
    "Anything in neither list is unclassified and is left alone until someone reads it in context.",
    "HKLM and HKCU are listed as literals because the decks that use them already write HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE and HKEY_CURRENT_USER out in full on the same slide, just never adjacent to the short form, which is the only thing the scanner can see.",
    "CFR is listed as a literal because it collides: in a compliance context it is the Code of Federal Regulations, but in COSC 332's Java decompiler list it is the tool named CFR. The scanner cannot tell which, and it expanded the tool name into the regulation. An acronym that means different things in two of these courses does not belong in this table at all.",
    "PMA is listed as a literal because every deck that uses it already writes out Practical Malware Analysis by Sikorski and Honig on the same Required Reading slide, just never next to the letters PMA, which is the only place the scanner can see it.",
    "Mixed-case acronyms (SoD, IoT, DDoS, IPsec, SaaS) were invisible to the original all-caps scanner and so were never classified at all. That is how SoD reached a slide title in MAC 247 session 2 with no expansion anywhere in the deck.",
    "A second pass classified 375 terms the first table never reached. Fifteen went to literal because they collide inside this corpus: ANSI is the standards body in one deck and the Windows character encoding in another, AP is the Kerberos exchange, accounts payable and a wireless access point in three different decks, and PAM is privileged access management in six decks and Linux pluggable authentication modules in one.",
    "UI is a literal because it collides and the collision corrupted a slide. In CVSS it is User Interaction, and the expander wrote 'user interface' inside the vector string itself, turning CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/... into something that is no longer a valid vector and is the exact string the exercise asks students to interpret."
  ],
